			"type": "mention",
			"via": "webmention"
		},
		{
			"id": 1270411,
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://www.bram.us/2021/09/16/cloud-function-to-generate-svg-sparklines/",
			"author": {
				"name": "Bramus!",
				"url": "https://www.bram.us/author/bramus/"
			},
			"date": "2021-09-16T19:17:51.000Z",
			"content": "<p><a href=\"https://www.bram.us/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/sparkline.png\"><img src=\"https://www.bram.us/wordpress/wp-content/plugins/native-lazyload/assets/images/placeholder.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"560\" height=\"233\" /></a></p><img src=\"https://www.bram.us/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/sparkline.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"560\" height=\"233\" />\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/zachleat\">Zach Leat</a> recently created a cloud function that dynamically generates sparklines. Under the hood sits the <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/sparkline-svg\">the <code>sparkline-svg</code> package</a> to generate the SVG. The datapoints themselves can be passed in via the URL.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Image URLs have the formats:</p>\n<ul><li><code>/[height]x[width]/[values]/</code></li>\n<li><code>/[height]x[width]/[values]/[color]/</code></li>\n</ul></blockquote>\n<p>The code is meant to be deployed as a <a href=\"https://www.bram.us/2019/12/28/a-netlify-serverless-function-in-one-tweet/\">Netlify Serverless Function</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.zachleat.com/web/sparklines/\">Every Fire needs a Sparkline →</a><br /><a href=\"https://github.com/11ty/api-sparkline\">Sparkline API (GitHub) →</a></p>\n\n<p>💡 This reminded me of Spark SVG <a href=\"https://www.bram.us/2013/01/18/spark-svg/\">which I covered back in 2013</a>.</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;embed src=\"https://sparksvg.me/line.svg?0,4,7,10,8,12,9,7,3,0\" type=\"image/svg+xml\"&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>It’s an SVG file with a snippet of JS contained inside it, a technique <a href=\"https://kryogenix.org/days/2012/12/30/simple-svg-sparklines/\">originally developed</a> by <a href=\"https://twitter.com/sil\">Stuart Langridge</a>. On load it will draw the sparkline, using the values as passed in the via the querystring.</p>\n<p>Upside with this approach is that you can host this SVG file on your own servers, without needing a cloud function. Downside however is that you need to include the SVGs via <code>&lt;embed&gt;</code> — <em>to allow script execution</em> — and require JS on the client to show them properly.</p>",
			"type": "mention",
			"via": "webmention"
		},
